        <h2>Understanding Your 10-Year Spanish Journey</h2>
        <p>The NLV is not a final destination—it's the gateway to permanent residency and eventually Spanish citizenship. This guide maps your entire journey from initial NLV approval through permanent residency and toward citizenship. Understanding this long-term pathway helps you make better decisions today.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Year 0: Initial NLV Approval and Arrival</h2>

      <div class="msv__timeline-block">
        <div class="msv__timeline-year">Upon Approval: What Happens</div>
        <ul>
          <li>Spanish consulate approves your NLV application</li>
          <li>You receive visa stamp in your passport (valid 1 year)</li>
          <li>You travel to Spain and present documents to immigration</li>
          <li>You collect your TIE card (residency card) at the immigration office—valid 1 year initially</li>
          <li>You register at your municipal padrón (local government residence register)</li>
          <li>You're now a legal resident of Spain</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p><strong>Key documents to keep:</strong> Original NLV visa stamp, TIE card (always carry), padrón registration certificate. These prove your legal residency status.</p>

      <p><strong>What you CANNOT do:</strong> Work (even self-employed), start a business, or earn income in Spain. The NLV is specifically non-lucrative.</p>

      <h2>Year 1: Your First Renewal</h2>

      <div class="msv__timeline-block">
        <div class="msv__timeline-year">Months 10-11 of Year 1: Begin Renewal Process</div>
        <ul>
          <li>60 days before your TIE expires, file renewal at immigration office (oficina de extranjería)</li>
          <li>Prepare same documentation as initial application: proof of income, health insurance, clean record</li>
          <li>Income requirement is IDENTICAL to initial application: €1,260-1,334/month minimum</li>
          <li>Consulate reviews renewal (typically 1-2 months)</li>
          <li>New TIE card is issued—this time valid for 2 YEARS (not just 1)</li>
          <li>You renew your NLV visa stamp in your passport</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p><strong>Critical detail:</strong> After the first renewal, your TIE is valid for 2 years. This means your next renewal won't be due until Year 3. Many applicants forget this and think they renew annually—they don't. You renew in Year 1, then Year 3, then Year 5.</p>

      <p><strong>Requirement consistency:</strong> The income requirement (€1,260-1,334/month) stays the same at every renewal. You never need more; you need to prove this amount continuously.</p>

      <h2>Years 2-4: Maintaining Your NLV Status</h2>

      <div class="msv__timeline-block">
        <div class="msv__timeline-year">Years 2-3: Your 2-Year TIE is Valid</div>
        <ul>
          <li>No renewal needed—your 2-year TIE covers you until Year 3</li>
          <li>Maintain income requirement: €1,260-1,334/month (documentation for tax purposes)</li>
          <li>Maintain health insurance: Required throughout NLV period</li>
          <li>Stay registered at padrón: Update if you move</li>
          <li>Spend 183+ days in Spain per calendar year: Required for renewal verification</li>
          <li>Avoid any criminal convictions: Would jeopardize your status</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__timeline-block">
        <div class="msv__timeline-year">Year 3: Your Second Renewal</div>
