        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>The Critical 183-Day Rule Explained</h2>

      <p>During your NLV years (0-5), you must spend at least 183 days per calendar year physically in Spain. This is one of the strict NLV requirements that consulates verify at every renewal.</p>

      <h3>How the 183-Day Rule Works</h3>

      <p><strong>What counts:</strong> Any day you're physically in Spain (even partial days). The day you arrive and day you depart both count.</p>

      <p><strong>How it's verified:</strong> Consulates check your passport stamps (entry/exit records). They may also review airline ticket records if your passport stamps are incomplete. In some cases, immigration officials ask direct questions: "Where were you last August?" Be consistent with your records.</p>

      <p><strong>Exceptions:</strong> There are no official exceptions. However, short trips outside Spain don't disqualify you—only if you're gone more than 182 days in a calendar year.</p>

      <p><strong>Example:</strong> You can leave Spain for 3 months (90 days) and still meet the 183-day requirement if you're back in Spain for the remaining 275 days. The rule is flexible—it's about your net days IN Spain.</p>

      <h3>Gray Area: What if You Don't Meet 183 Days?</h3>

      <p>If consulates discover you didn't spend 183 days in Spain in a renewal year, your renewal application can be DENIED. This is serious. However, consulates have some discretion:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>If you're at 170-182 days (close), consulates may approve with warning</li>
        <li>If you're significantly short (e.g., 150 days), expect denial</li>
        <li>Medical emergencies or family crises may be considered (document these)</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Strategy:</strong> Track your days carefully. Use calendar apps to log entry/exit dates. If you travel frequently, keep detailed records. Consulates are stricter on this than on other requirements.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <div class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>The 183-day rule is strict at renewal.</strong> Plan your travel carefully during NLV years. If you spend more than 6 months outside Spain, your renewal is at risk. Once you reach permanent residency (Year 5), this enforcement relaxes—but you still need Spain as your primary residence.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Year 5: The Major Milestone - Permanent Residency</h2>

      <p>After 5 consecutive years as a legal resident (your NLV doesn't have to be continuously valid, just your residency status), you become eligible for permanent residency. This is a major status change and one of the most important milestones in your Spanish journey.</p>

      <h3>What Changes Fundamentally at Year 5</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Income requirement DISAPPEARS:</strong> You no longer need to prove €1,260-1,334/month. Zero income required.</li>
        <li><strong>Residency card valid 10 years:</strong> Instead of renewing every 2 years, your next card doesn't need renewal for a full decade</li>
        <li><strong>WORK RIGHTS UNLOCK:</strong> This is the game-changer. You can now legally work (employed, self-employed, business owner)</li>
        <li><strong>The "non-lucrative" restriction is lifted:</strong> You can earn income in Spain without losing your status</li>
        <li><strong>183-day rule enforcement relaxes:</strong> You still need Spain as primary residence, but enforcement is lighter</li>
        <li><strong>Social benefits expand:</strong> Access to more Spanish social welfare programs</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Permanent Residency Application Process (Form EX-17)</h3>

      <p>File at your local immigration office (oficina de extranjería) starting in Year 5:</p>

      <ol style="list-style: decimal; padding-left: 1.75rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        <li><strong>Prove 5 years legal residency:</strong> Padrón records, TIE renewal history, any documentation showing continuous residency</li>
        <li><strong>Show financial self-sufficiency:</strong> Less strict than NLV, but demonstrate stability (tax returns, bank statements, income sources). You don't need €1,260/month—just show you can support yourself</li>
        <li><strong>Valid health insurance:</strong> Still required (though some permanent residents use Spanish public healthcare instead)</li>
        <li><strong>Clean criminal record:</strong> No convictions since arrival in Spain</li>
        <li><strong>Complete Form EX-17:</strong> Official permanent residency application (available at immigration office or online)</li>
        <li><strong>Submit documents bundle:</strong> All originals and certified copies (specific requirements vary by region)</li>
      </ol>

      <p><strong>Processing timeline:</strong> 1-2 months typically. Some regions process faster; some slower. Check with your specific immigration office.</p>

      <p><strong>Success rate:</strong> High. If you've maintained NLV requirements for 5 years, permanent residency is almost certain to be approved.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--green">
        <div class="msv__alert-icon">✓</div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>Work rights unlock at permanent residency:</strong> This is transformative. Many NLV holders wait until Year 5 to start businesses or take employment because now they can legally earn income in Spain without losing their status. This is when "non-lucrative" NLV holders often become entrepreneurs or employees. It's a pivotal moment in your Spanish residency journey.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Years 5-10: Permanent Residency Status</h2>

      <h3>What Obligations Change (What You No Longer Need)</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>No income proof:</strong> Requirement drops to €0. You can be fully unemployed and maintain status</li>
        <li><strong>Health insurance no longer mandatory:</strong> Optional but highly recommended (use private insurance or Spanish public system)</li>
        <li><strong>Renewal every 2 years ends:</strong> Your 10-year residency card doesn't expire for a full decade</li>
        <li><strong>Padrón registration relaxes slightly:</strong> Still required, but less stringent verification</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What Obligations Remain (Critical)</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Maintain Spain as primary residence:</strong> You cannot establish permanent residency elsewhere. Spain must be your main home</li>
        <li><strong>Stay registered at padrón:</strong> Still required; update if you move within Spain</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid criminal convictions:</strong> Serious crime can still result in permanent residency cancellation</li>
        <li><strong>File Spanish income tax returns:</strong> If you earn income in Spain, you must report it to tax authorities (and pay taxes)</li>
        <li><strong>Notify authorities of changes:</strong> Change of address, legal status changes, etc.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Work Opportunities Now Available</h3>

      <p>With permanent residency, you can legally:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Become employed:</strong> Get hired by any Spanish company in any field. No restrictions</li>
        <li><strong>Start a business:</strong> Register as self-employed (autónomo) or create a company (S.L. or S.A.)</li>
        <li><strong>Hire employees:</strong> Your business can employ other people legally</li>
        <li><strong>Work remotely:</strong> For foreign companies with no Spanish office—fully legal with permanent residency</li>
        <li><strong>Buy business licenses:</strong> Open restaurants, shops, services, etc.</li>
        <li><strong>Invest and own property:</strong> Business property, investment property with full legal rights</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Many successful NLV holders strategically wait until Year 5 when permanent residency is approved, then immediately launch businesses they've been planning. The legal certainty of permanent residency is valuable for entrepreneurs.</p>

      <h2>Year 10: Path to Spanish Citizenship and Naturalization</h2>

      <p>After 10 consecutive years of legal residency, you become eligible for Spanish citizenship through naturalization (naturalización). This grants you a Spanish passport and full EU citizenship rights.</p>

      <h3>Requirements for Spanish Citizenship</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>10 years legal residency:</strong> From your initial NLV approval date. All years count—even if your individual TIE cards expired and were renewed</li>
        <li><strong>Spanish language proficiency:</strong> Pass DELE B1 exam (intermediate Spanish). DELE B2 is safer if you want margin. A1 or A2 doesn't qualify</li>
        <li><strong>Spanish civics/Constitution knowledge:</strong> Pass exam on Spanish Constitution, civil rights, administrative structure, basic history. Official curriculum provided</li>
        <li><strong>Clean criminal record:</strong> No serious convictions during residency period</li>
        <li><strong>Integration into Spanish society:</strong> Show effort to integrate (employment, volunteer work, community involvement, Spanish language use). This is vague but demonstrated by your 10-year presence</li>
        <li><strong>Spanish name not required:</strong> Some people Hispanicize their names; it's optional</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The Citizenship Application Process</h3>

      <ol style="list-style: decimal; padding-left: 1.75rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
        <li><strong>Prepare for language exam:</strong> Take DELE B1 course (3-6 months typical). Study Spanish civics simultaneously</li>
        <li><strong>Pass DELE B1 exam:</strong> Administered by Spanish education ministry. Official exam with listening, reading, writing, speaking components</li>
        <li><strong>Pass Spanish civics exam:</strong> Government test on Constitution, rights, structure. Less difficult than language exam</li>
        <li><strong>Gather residency documentation:</strong> Padrón records for all 10 years, TIE renewal history, tax returns, employment records</li>
        <li><strong>File citizenship application:</strong> At Dirección General de Registros y Notariado (official citizenship office). Form and documents submitted</li>
        <li><strong>Await decision:</strong> 1-2 years processing (longest wait). Government reviews all documents, interviews applicant sometimes</li>
        <li><strong>Take oath of allegiance:</strong> If approved, take oath of loyalty to Spanish Constitution and monarchy</li>
        <li><strong>Receive Spanish passport:</strong> Apply for Spanish passport at police station. You're now a Spanish citizen</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>What Spanish Citizenship Grants You</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spanish passport:</strong> Travel with Spanish travel document (no longer dependent on your original country's passport)</li>
        <li><strong>Right to vote:</strong> Can vote in municipal, regional, and national Spanish elections</li>
        <li><strong>Right to run for office:</strong> Can become a politician, mayor, judge, etc.</li>
        <li><strong>EU freedom of movement:</strong> As Spanish citizen, you have full EU rights (work, live, study anywhere in EU)</li>
        <li><strong>No residency requirements:</strong> Can leave Spain permanently; citizenship doesn't require living in Spain</li>
        <li><strong>Spanish social benefits:</strong> Full access to all Spanish welfare, healthcare, education</li>
        <li><strong>Property rights:</strong> Spanish citizens have certain rights non-citizens don't (though most are academic)</li>
        <li><strong>Dual citizenship:</strong> Spain allows dual citizenship—keep your original nationality</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The DELE B1 Language Requirement</h3>

      <p><strong>Level B1 means:</strong> Intermediate Spanish proficiency. You can understand main points of clear conversation, express yourself fluently on familiar topics, deal with unexpected situations.</p>

      <p><strong>Typical timeline to B1:</strong> 6-12 months of serious study (if starting from A2). Classes, private tutors, or immersion learning all work. Many NLV holders reach B1 within their 5 years of residency just through living in Spain.</p>

      <p><strong>Exam cost:</strong> €200-250 for official DELE exam. Preparation courses €500-2,000.</p>

      <p><strong>Exam format:</strong> 4 sections (reading, writing, listening, speaking). Each section tested separately. You need decent scores in all sections to pass (roughly 60%+ in each).</p>

