        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Clear answers to long-term pathway questions on residency progression.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>How long does it take to go from NLV to permanent residency?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>After 5 consecutive years as a legal resident (your NLV doesn't have to be continuously valid, just your residency status), you can apply for permanent residency. The application itself processes in 1-2 months typically. So total time: 5 years of NLV + 1-2 months application processing = approximately 5 years and 1-2 months from initial NLV approval to permanent residency approval.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What are the key changes when you reach year 5 and permanent residency?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Major changes: (1) No more income requirement (€0 vs €1,260-1,334/month); (2) Residency card valid 10 years (vs renewing every 2 years); (3) Work rights unlock—you can now legally work, be self-employed, start businesses (the 'non-lucrative' restriction is gone); (4) Less strict travel restrictions; (5) Access to more Spanish social benefits. Year 5 is transformative because work rights finally become available.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How does the 183-day rule work during NLV years?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>During NLV years (0-5), you must spend at least 183 days per calendar year in Spain. This is verified at every renewal through passport stamps and airline records. Consulates check this carefully. Short trips outside Spain don't disqualify you—only if you're gone more than 182 days per calendar year. After permanent residency, while you still must maintain Spain as your primary residence, enforcement of the 183-day rule is lighter.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>When can I start working in Spain on NLV?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>You cannot work until permanent residency (Year 5+). The NLV is strictly non-lucrative. Working on NLV violates visa conditions and can result in visa cancellation. However, many NLV holders wait strategically until Year 5 when permanent residency is approved, then immediately start businesses or employment. This is the legal pathway for income-earning work in Spain—you cannot bypass this by working remotely for foreign companies while on NLV (though enforcement varies).</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What's required to apply for permanent residency at year 5?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>To apply for permanent residency: prove 5 years legal residency (padrón records, TIE history), show financial self-sufficiency (less strict than NLV—just show stability, not a specific amount), valid health insurance, clean criminal record, and complete Form EX-17. Processing takes 1-2 months. The requirements are lighter than initial NLV because you've already proven stability by being resident 5 years and maintaining NLV requirements.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I become a Spanish citizen after NLV and permanent residency?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. After 10 years of legal residency, you become eligible for Spanish citizenship through naturalization. Requirements: prove 10 years residency, pass DELE B1 Spanish exam (intermediate level), pass Spanish civics test, clean criminal record, and show integration into Spanish society. Processing takes 1-2 years. Upon approval, you receive a Spanish passport and gain full EU citizenship rights, voting rights, and freedom to live anywhere in the EU.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
